<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Epimenides</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Epimenides of Knossos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Epimenides was a semi-legendary Cretan seer and poet, traditionally dated to the 7th or 6th century BCE. Greek tradition says he purified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> of the pollution caused by the killing of the followers of Cylon, and stories told of his sleep of fifty-seven years in a cave. A line attributed to him, that Cretans are always liars, is quoted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> letter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus">Titus</a> and gave rise to the liar paradox.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epimenides.jpg" alt="Epimenides" width="256" height="356" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epimenides.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Epimenides came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaistos">Phaistos</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. While tending his father's sheep he is said to have fallen asleep for fifty-seven years in a cave sacred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, and to have woken with the gift of prophecy. According to Diogenes Laërtius, he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythagoras">Pythagoras</a> in Crete, and the two went to the Cave of Ida. His countrymen later honoured him as a god and held that he lived nearly three hundred years. Another story says he was captured in a war between the Spartans and the Knossians and put to death because he refused to prophesy favourably for his captors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Diogenes Laërtius relates that when a pestilence raged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> the Pythonic oracle told the Athenians to fetch Epimenides from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. He set sheep on Mars Hill and had altars raised where they lay down, which was held to explain the anonymous altars of the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> writes that he purified Athens after the pollution brought by the Alcmeonidae, and that his knowledge of sacrifice and his reform of funeral practice helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a> in reforming the Athenian state. He would accept only a branch of the sacred olive and a promise of lasting friendship between Athens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>.</p><p>Athenaeus also mentions him in connection with the self-sacrifice of Aristodemus and Cratinus, a story some in antiquity thought to be fiction. He was also said to have prophesied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> on military matters.</p><p>Pausanias reports that when Epimenides died his skin was found covered with tattooed writing, which was odd because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> reserved tattooing for slaves. Some modern scholars have taken this as evidence that he was heir to the shamanic religions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. His skin was said to be kept at the court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Several prose and verse works, now lost, were attributed to him, among them a theogony, an epic on the Argonautic expedition, prose works on purifications and sacrifices, a cosmogony, oracles and a work on the laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. His Cretica is thought to be quoted twice in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>. The Epistle to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus">Titus</a> cites a line calling the Cretans always liars and attributes it to a prophet of their own, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-of-alexandria">Clement of Alexandria</a> identified as Epimenides. The saying created a paradox of self-reference like the liar paradox, known as the Epimenides paradox. James Rendel Harris argued that the passage in Acts 17:28, in Him we live and move and have our being, derives from the same poem.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Epimenides” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The seer <b>Epimenides</b>, traditionally said to be a Knossian, was remembered for purifying Athens after the sacrilege of the Cylonian conspiracy, and a line attributed to him, that Cretans are always liars, is quoted in the New Testament letter to Titus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.3,25.16" aria-label="Knossos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Epimenides"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4663 4540h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4663 4540h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.3,25.16">What was here? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
